WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.620
As soon as a .50-caliber round hits water,

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.110
typically it ricochets.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:12.310
So it's highly unlikely
that that would penetrate

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:13.560
any kind of steel hull.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.510
My name is Adm. Jamie Foggo,

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.900
and I served in the Navy
for almost 40 years.

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.920
When I retired, I took over as dean

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.130
of the Center for Maritime
Strategy of the Navy League

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.660
here in Washington, DC.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.400
Today, we're going to be looking at

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.570
warship scenes from movies

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.762
and judging how realistic they are.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:41.142
King Kong covers his
ears, not unexpectedly.

00:00:41.142 --> 00:00:44.015
Hearing protection and
hearing conservation

00:00:44.015 --> 00:00:46.170
is a big deal in the United States Navy.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.360
And if you're a gunner's mate

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.960
or anybody that's been on a ship

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.750
firing a small-caliber weapon

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:53.710
to a 5-inch gun, it's loud.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:56.580
So no doubt the only
damage we did to King Kong

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.288
was probably rupture his eardrums.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.968
Now, a US aircraft carrier

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:08.159
displaces 90,000 tons.

00:01:08.159 --> 00:01:12.760
According to Google, King
Kong weighs 50,000 tons.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.990
Now that's a heck of a lot of weight,

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.630
half the weight of the
carrier, dropping on its deck.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:18.530
But we make our aircraft carriers

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.820
to be battle-ready and battle-hardened.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.403
So my money's on the carrier

00:01:22.403 --> 00:01:24.819
as far as King Kong and his 50,000 tons

00:01:24.819 --> 00:01:26.770
landing and not breaking
through that deck.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.315
It's not part of our
tactical training in the Navy

00:01:36.315 --> 00:01:38.830
to fight either one of
those two creatures.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:40.110
A lot of weapons used.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.070
I see deck guns,

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.411
I see anti-submarine rockets,

00:01:45.411 --> 00:01:47.002
more or less realistic.

00:01:47.002 --> 00:01:49.660
Now, when they launch
something called a Hedgehog

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.740
from the starboard side
of one of the ships,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.810
we don't have those anymore,

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.100
but some of our allies
and partners do in Europe.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.800
This is a weapon that's typically
used against submarines.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.810
We have a more sophisticated
and modern method.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.830
It's an anti-submarine rocket.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.750
So typically a Mark 54
lightweight torpedo.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.280
It's launched into the air.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.900
When it hits the water, it separates,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.960
and that torpedo behaves
then just like a torpedo.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:17.310
And the reason you do that
is to go after a submarine,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.635
not necessarily to go after Godzilla.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.280
Godzilla grabs one of the
ships and rolls it over.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.130
That's pretty hard to do
when you're talking about

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:35.900
tens of thousands of tons of displacement

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.110
and a hull form that is
designed to take bad weather.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.940
Now, for an aircraft carrier

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:44.390
that displaces 90,000
tons, highly unlikely.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:47.100
For realism, pretty fictional here.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.008
1 out of 10.

00:02:48.008 --> 00:02:50.000
Cradle lowered.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.493
All right, now ram the projectile.

00:02:51.493 --> 00:02:52.326
Easy.

00:02:53.721 --> 00:02:55.700
When you ram that powder, you ram it easy.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:56.750
Real easy.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.512
This is one of the most accurate movies

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:04.090
on how you load a 16-inch
projectile into a 16-inch barrel.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.090
That projectile weighs 2,000 pounds.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:10.800
So you're essentially
loading a Volkswagen Beetle

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.410
into the breech of that gun
and firing it at the enemy.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.720
And you notice that the projectile,

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.320
it's followed by these powder charges.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.330
This was a very accurate representation

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.620
of how you load that gun.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.650
There are spark-proof tools,

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.600
and everybody is very
careful, because, you know,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.710
that powder is obviously
explosive and volatile,

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.160
and any mistake or any spark or any flame

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.740
could set it off and blow up that turret.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.840
204 degrees, Calloway.

00:03:39.648 --> 00:03:40.830
Sailor 1: 204 degrees.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.130
Sailor 2: 204 degrees.

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:46.860
This very shiny brass
device, that's a gun sight.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.130
And that's to allow the gun
captain to take a look out

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.680
and see the direction that he's firing in.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.240
That's the way that we used to do it,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.120
before we had these very
sophisticated fire-control radars

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.017
and gun directors that are
on top of the turrets today.

00:04:03.017 --> 00:04:04.592
All right, fire in the hole.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.610
I got a chuckle out of the
fact that Tommy Lee Jones

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.950
runs toward the 16-inch gun
turret just before it's fired.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.550
We had on the battleships procedures

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:21.099
to prevent sailors from being topside

00:04:21.099 --> 00:04:23.812
in proximity to those turrets
when they fired the guns,

00:04:23.812 --> 00:04:26.410
because the overpressure of the blast

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.270
blew him back, you know,
a dozen or so feet.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.700
And that's probably fairly accurate.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.110
Even today, with our missile systems,

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:38.380
if you're launching from
the foredeck of a destroyer,

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:39.890
nobody's up there when you're launching,

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:43.150
because there's going to
be a lot of flash to bang

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:44.850
as the weapon comes out of the tube,

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.110
and you don't want anybody to get hurt.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.277
I'd give it a 7 out of 10.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:58.040
"Battleship" happens to be
one of my favorite movies.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.910
These are World War II-era ships.

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:03.520
They're not part of the
active fleet anymore,

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.990
but these ships are made to fight.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:08.360
And when you're on one
of those battleships

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.700
and you have a belt of armor around you

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.480
12 to 17 inches thick

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:19.478
and 50,000 tons of steel to protect you,

00:05:19.478 --> 00:05:21.005
that's pretty reassuring.

00:05:21.005 --> 00:05:22.113
Drop forward anchor.

00:05:22.113 --> 00:05:22.946
What?

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.330
The officer of the deck on the bridge

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:37.370
uses the anchor on the battleship

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:42.370
to avoid getting hit by
some of the aliens' weapons.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:43.740
Frankly, that's pretty hokey.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.450
The amount of chain on a
battleship or an aircraft carrier

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.600
connected to the anchor is pretty long.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.470
It's probably at least 1,000 feet.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.210
But we don't use anchors for that reason.

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.580
We use anchors to moor the ship in port.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.791
And this is in the Pacific
Ocean, which is really deep.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.157
One thing that the
viewers might not notice

00:06:12.157 --> 00:06:14.660
is it's so powerful that the ship

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:17.870
actually moves sideways several yards.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:20.910
When you fire a broadside,
it can move significantly,

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.210
and you have to do a
course correction there.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:24.950
I'd probably give it a 2.

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:27.170
I think Hollywood took
a little poetic license

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:28.603
on the anchor in this case.

00:06:30.115 --> 00:06:31.731
Torpedo!

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:40.190
This clip is tough to watch.

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:43.540
Probably the greatest
fear that any sailor has

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:46.460
is being stuck in a
compartment filling with water

00:06:46.460 --> 00:06:48.710
and being unable to get out.

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:50.701
The ship is rolling over.

00:06:50.701 --> 00:06:53.184
Ordnance is exploding on board.

00:06:53.184 --> 00:06:56.210
And if you get out, that's great,

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.520
but then you're going to jump

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.510
into the freezing waters of the Atlantic.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.220
And you're still wearing
your boots and your kit,

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:05.470
which is, if you're not a good swimmer,

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:07.420
it's pretty hard not to sink.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:16.230
One of the enemies
against their escape route

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.840
are these watertight doors.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:20.207
The watertight doors are heavy,

00:07:20.207 --> 00:07:23.048
and they're created to contain the water

00:07:23.048 --> 00:07:25.600
that might be leaking into a compartment.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.000
Or, if there was some kind
of a catastrophic explosion,

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:30.550
the reason that they're heavy

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:35.340
is to contain that blast
overpressure in the compartment.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.420
Now, most doors on ships or submarines

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:40.320
have two kinds of latching mechanisms.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.260
There's something called a latch or a dog.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:43.630
We call it a dog.

00:07:43.630 --> 00:07:47.340
And when you're in a normal steaming,

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:48.932
you just push the door shut,

00:07:48.932 --> 00:07:52.010
and it latches the same
way a screen door does.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:53.770
In the event that you're at

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.570
a heightened readiness condition,

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:58.980
you will crank that
wheel to lock that door.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.570
And the only way to get it open

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.820
is to open it counterclockwise.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.670
So you can imagine, you're
at an awkward angle,

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:06.830
your hands are slippery

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.070
because there might be
oil in the compartment,

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:12.270
there's water in the compartment,
and you're panicking.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:13.970
You know, it's a life-and-death situation.

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:14.960
You're trying to get out.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:17.913
I think the realism factor
here is a 10 out of 10.

00:08:21.569 --> 00:08:23.587
Second torpedo to starboard beam!

00:08:23.587 --> 00:08:24.820
Bearing 120!

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:26.678
Right hard rudder, hard over!

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:32.260
"Greyhound" is an
absolutely phenomenal movie.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:37.260
It tells and depicts with
great historical accuracy.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.340
The Germans are depicted in the movie

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:42.540
as deploying the wolfpack tactics,

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:46.012
and you see two torpedoes coming
from different directions.

00:08:46.012 --> 00:08:48.660
You know, that's more
than one submarine firing,

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:51.903
and these tactics were
extremely effective.

00:08:51.903 --> 00:08:52.986
Belay that, belay that!

00:08:52.986 --> 00:08:54.010
Meet her. Meet her!

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.520
Belay, meet her, meet her!

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:58.160
So, what you see on the bridge of the ship

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:00.300
is an attack on the destroyer.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.680
You know, I think that was very realistic.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:05.460
And you see the wake of the
torpedo coming towards you.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:08.380
The propellers do
something we call cavitate.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:11.350
They create bubbles as they're
going through the water,

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:14.050
and that creates a trail
behind the torpedo.

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:17.490
And so a sharp lookout
can pick that up early,

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.960
and it gives the commanding
officer an advantage

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.370
to conduct those drastic
evasive maneuvers.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.870
If it was nighttime,

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.560
a lot of times it's even
easier to see the torpedo

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.810
because of the
phosphorescence in the water.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:32.890
It kind of glows as it's coming at you.

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:34.990
A lot of these torpedoes
were electric-powered.

00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:37.650
They had batteries and
electric motors in them,

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.440
and they would go towards the target.

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:42.920
Nowadays, we have torpedoes
that are propelled by fuel

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:44.670
and have a very long range.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.040
And so it was a different game back then,

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:49.307
but in this case, absolutely accurate.

00:09:56.895 --> 00:09:59.170
As far as the maneuverability is concerned

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.560
of that destroyer, you
have to keep in mind,

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.010
this is not a battleship
that displaces 50,000 tons.

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:07.951
These are lighter ships.

00:10:07.951 --> 00:10:11.067
Nowadays a heavy destroyer
in the United States Navy's

00:10:11.067 --> 00:10:13.230
about 7,800, 8,000 tons.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:16.160
The propulsion systems
on these destroyers,

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.710
the engines and the
propellers and the rudders,

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:21.960
are made for speed and maneuverability,

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:25.150
particularly because in the
second battle of the Atlantic,

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:26.950
they were not only going
after surface ships,

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:28.180
they were going after submarines.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.605
And that means you've got to
be able to turn on a dime.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.240
The rounds that actually hit the water

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.010
or penetrate the water,

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:43.903
whether or not they actually
do any damage, it depends.

00:10:43.903 --> 00:10:47.810
As soon as a .50-caliber round hits water,

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:49.500
typically it ricochets.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.930
If it does go beneath the waterline,

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:54.480
you'll lose velocity very quickly.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.655
So it's highly unlikely
that that would penetrate

00:10:57.655 --> 00:10:58.710
any kind of steel hull.

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:00.269
But again, you never know.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:09.120
So, he has to surface,
because water's coming in.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:10.800
And then while he's on the surface,

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:14.250
they finish him off in
pretty much a brutal manner.

00:11:14.250 --> 00:11:17.330
But you see what looks
like anti-aircraft guns

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:19.630
that are lowered to the deck-plate level.

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:22.840
As this projectile went in and it blew up,

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:24.680
it may have caused secondary explosions.

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:27.760
First there's an initial
boom, and then a massive boom.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:31.930
There's so much oil and fuel and weapons

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:34.370
on board that submarine that, you know,

00:11:34.370 --> 00:11:37.520
once it took that initial
penetration of the hull

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.060
and those shaped charges
went in, it exploded.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:43.230
This movie gets absolutely
top marks from me.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.833
As far as realism, 10 out of 10.

00:11:54.545 --> 00:11:56.758
Are we absolutely sure of our position?

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.785
Yes, sir. An exact satellite fix.

00:11:59.785 --> 00:12:03.530
It's Her Majesty's Ship Devonshire

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:07.200
that thinks she is operating
in international waters.

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.660
The bad guys have somehow interfered

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:13.310
with the navigation system of that ship,

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:18.090
and she's actually
operating one mile inside

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:19.537
Chinese territorial waters.

00:12:19.537 --> 00:12:21.190
Devonshire doesn't know that.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:25.810
Now, that's not unrealistic
in digital warfare today.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:29.280
We just had a situation in the Black Sea

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.171
where somebody spoofed

00:12:32.171 --> 00:12:35.062
the automatic information system

00:12:35.062 --> 00:12:39.323
of a couple of NATO
warships and depicted them

00:12:39.323 --> 00:12:43.473
off the coast of Sevastopol in the Crimea,

00:12:43.473 --> 00:12:45.410
where there's a Russian naval base.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.047
That's dangerous activity.

00:12:47.047 --> 00:12:50.520
This scenario is fairly realistic.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:54.360
English is the international
language for communication

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.130
in airspace or sea space.

00:12:57.130 --> 00:13:01.510
But many times somebody
on the bridge of a ship

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:02.810
may not speak English,

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.660
so we have other methods
for communication.

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.310
We have flashing lights,

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.440
we have signal flags,

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:13.599
and we have agreements to avoid mistakes.

00:13:13.599 --> 00:13:15.399
Sound the general alarm.

00:13:15.399 --> 00:13:18.151
[alarm blaring]
The ship has been overflown

00:13:18.151 --> 00:13:20.230
by two Chinese planes.

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:23.120
We believe they have hostile intent.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:26.040
The commanding officer
directs a general alarm.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:29.800
So that is a call to action
stations, battle stations.

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:33.110
There's also a series of
red lights that go off.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:36.550
Typically on a surface ship at night,

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:38.530
you are in a darkened-ship status,

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.820
so there's very low light in compartments.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:46.307
There are key points
throughout the passageways

00:13:46.307 --> 00:13:48.860
that are lit, but not
necessarily red lights.

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:52.177
So there's a little bit of
Hollywood poetic license here.

00:13:59.973 --> 00:14:01.977
The unrealistic part of this scenario

00:14:01.977 --> 00:14:04.800
is this thing that looks like a drill.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.610
Penetrating the hull
of a warship like that

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.360
will not sink the warship.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:11.380
Now, in this case, the
bad guys figured out

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:14.210
that by boring through
the hull at a weak point

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:17.340
and going in along the length
or along the keel of the ship

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:19.230
to go through several compartments,

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.420
and by that time,

00:14:20.420 --> 00:14:23.990
HMS Devonshire was overwhelmed
with displacement of water,

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:24.970
and it sunk.

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:28.043
So as far as realism, a 1 out of 10.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.520
Sir, I'm picking up mobile bandwidth

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.987
low-latency transmissions.
Could be a UAV data link.

00:14:34.987 --> 00:14:37.090
You know, "The Last Ship" is a show

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:38.310
that I'm very familiar with.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.560
In the clip, they talk about a UAV.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:43.490
It stands for unmanned aerial vehicle.

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:47.320
So it's a intelligence surveillance
and reconnaissance tool.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:48.740
In the last decade,

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:52.540
we've seen UAVs evolve
from just being a sensor

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:55.520
that takes a video or a picture

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:57.680
and sends it back to the mothership

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:00.940
to UAVs with real weapons on board.

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:02.230
Enable spy radar.

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:03.450
But sir, if the Russians pick it up,

00:15:03.450 --> 00:15:04.600
they'll track our bearing.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:05.943
We've got 30 seconds,
then we shut it down.

00:15:05.943 --> 00:15:06.776
Sir?

00:15:06.776 --> 00:15:07.609
I gave an order.

00:15:07.609 --> 00:15:12.410
The spy radar, that is part
of the Aegis weapon system

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:14.120
or radar system on the ship.

00:15:14.120 --> 00:15:18.290
And the radar that is
looking out on the horizon,

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.770
over the horizon, in the air, and on land

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:25.410
for 100-plus miles is the SPY-1 radar.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:26.815
That's the best radar in the world,

00:15:26.815 --> 00:15:31.797
and it gives us essentially
100% situational awareness.

00:15:31.797 --> 00:15:34.120
I had four of these
destroyers at my disposal

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:35.811
when I served in Europe,

00:15:35.811 --> 00:15:37.452
and they were absolutely terrific.

00:15:37.452 --> 00:15:39.897
There's really two areas of the ship

00:15:39.897 --> 00:15:43.800
that are areas where
the ship is controlled.

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:45.030
One is the bridge.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:46.390
So that's where you drive the ship.

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:48.970
That's where you control speed and rudder

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:51.120
and course and direction.

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:54.060
The real heart of the ship for fighting

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.070
is in the combat information center.

00:15:56.070 --> 00:16:00.320
You see the sailors taking
direction from their skipper

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:03.300
and carrying out that
direction effectively.

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:04.750
What I like about the series

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:06.570
and what I like about the scene is

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:10.578
it's the calm professionalism
that you would expect.

00:16:10.578 --> 00:16:11.660
I give it high marks.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:14.630
I give it, for realism
and professionalism,

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:16.730
I give it a 9 out of 10.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:18.193
General quarters.

00:16:18.193 --> 00:16:19.026
Sound general quarters.

00:16:19.026 --> 00:16:19.859
All engines ahead full.

00:16:19.859 --> 00:16:20.709
Aye aye, sir.

00:16:22.894 --> 00:16:25.844
[whistling]

00:16:25.844 --> 00:16:29.480
You know, you hear whistle
signals from the bridge.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:32.410
In this particular case, these
guys are at battle stations.

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:34.010
They want to get people's attention.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:38.900
And in the Surface Navy today,
tradition will have it that

00:16:38.900 --> 00:16:41.670
when somebody wants to address the crew

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:43.540
over the general announcing system,

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:46.850
oftentimes the boatswain
will blow his pipe

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.766
to get the attention of everybody.

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:52.741
Stand by to roll depth charges.

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:02.520
It speeds towards the submarine,
the submarine submerges,

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:04.810
it drops a series of depth charges.

00:17:04.810 --> 00:17:06.660
So those depth charges are in canisters,

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:09.820
they look like 50-gallon drums,
on the stern of the ship,

00:17:09.820 --> 00:17:12.260
and they were actually rolled off the ship

00:17:12.260 --> 00:17:17.100
after a gunner's mate
set the charge to explode

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:19.630
and set the depth that
it would explode at.

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:20.913
You had to estimate at

00:17:20.913 --> 00:17:24.540
how deep you thought
that submarine had dove

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:27.940
after she went from the surface
down to try to evade you.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:30.990
And it was a bit of a
guessing game, but, you know,

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:33.340
there are limits to how
deep the submarine can go.

00:17:33.340 --> 00:17:36.780
For historical accuracy, 10 out of 10.

00:17:36.780 --> 00:17:37.920
Thanks for watching.

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:40.743
And if you enjoyed the video,
click on the link above.

